<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n74699" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀπωρ-ινός</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ή</span>,
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">όν</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>of</i>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀπώρα</span> or <i>late summer,</i>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀστέρ᾽ ὀπωρινῷ ἐναλίγκιον,</span> i.
			e. Sirius, the star whose rising marked the beginning of that season
			(v. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀπώρα</span>), <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				5.5
			</span></i> ; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἦμαρ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					16.385
				</span></i>
			 ; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">βορέης</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					21.346
				</span></i>
			, <i><span class="en">
				Od.
				5.328
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὄμβρος</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Hes.
					<u>Op.</u>
					674
				</span></i>
			, <i><span class="en">
				677
			</span></i> ; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὄρχατοι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					E.
					<u>Fr.</u>
					896
				</span></i>
			 ; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δέλφαξ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Ar.
					<u>Fr.</u>
					506.4
				</span></i>
			 ; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πυλαία</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					<u>SIG</u>
					239
				</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				<u>C</u>
				31
			</span></i>, al. (Delph., <span class="entity">iv B. C.</span>). [In <i><span class="en">
				Hom.
			</span></i> the last syll. is always long (by position in <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				21.346
			</span></i>), and the penult. is long also, metri gr.: when the ult. is
			short, the penult. also is short, as in <i><span class="en">
				Hes.
				<u>Op.</u>
				674
			</span></i> ; in 
				Att.
			
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ῐ</span> always ; cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μετοπωρινός.]</span></div>
